The idea is to bring ocean model data and in-situ observations into one interactive workspace, instead of making users work with separate tools.
In our prototype, we use HYCOM as the model source and provide ocean variables such as temperature, salinity, oxygen and chlorophyll for exploration.
Users can explore the ocean in 3D and top-down views, change the region, variable, time and depth, and see the ocean field together with Argo floats.
When a user selects an Argo float, they can open its profile, trajectory and metadata, and move into the model-versus-observation comparison.
Here, the system checks whether the model and observation are actually comparable — including variable, units, spatial proximity, timestamp and depth.
We also make depth matching transparent. For example, if an observation is at a depth that does not exactly match a model level, the interface identifies it as interpolated, instead of hiding that processing step.
We then show the model and observation values together, their difference, and validation measures such as RMSE and Bias.
Our main innovation is traceability.
We show the data provenance, quality information, and matching process, so researchers can understand not only the result, but also how that result was produced.
We also demonstrate the effect of localized EnOI data assimilation through our Raw Model and Assimilated Model views.
Finally, Sagar-Drishti has two modes. Researcher mode provides detailed analysis, quality information and comparison tools, while Education mode provides a simpler guided experience for learning about the ocean using real-world observation concepts.
So our key idea is simple: explore the ocean in 3D, compare the model with observations, make the comparison traceable, and then visualize the effect of data assimilation.
